    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82998.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "No, it is exciting. And I think we see a lot of young candidates running across the country. And look, it's not like Gen Z was waiting in Congress. I'm the oldest at Gen Z or it can be. We just got old enough to run. But I think the significance here is that the first chance we got, we jumped at the opportunity. Because we want to be a part of the solution. We want to be at the table. We don't want to be the only people at the table. But Congress does not look like our country right now. Gen Z and millennials make up a third of the country. We're nowhere near a third of government, both local and federal. When any more people to run for office, yes, Congress, but also city council, county commission, these seats that really are, they're not lower, they're closer to the people and closer to the decisions that matter on a daily basis.",
